          "summary": "Step 2 \u2014 embedded code region creation and collapse \u2014 is fully and accurately delivered. EmbeddedFenceDetector provides pure fence-detection/serialization logic; EmbeddedCodeRegionManager performs real FlowDocument segment surgery and is genuinely wired into MainWindow.PromptTextBox_TextChanged behind a correct reentrancy guard (the guard prevents infinite recursion from the nested TextChanged events caused by Document.Blocks.Clear(), it does not disable detection \u2014 the outer call still runs the conversion loop before returning). EmbeddedCodeRegionBlock is a real interactive Border/ToggleButton control reusing CopyableCodeBlockBuilder\u0027s Consolas/CodeSurface/CodeText convention verbatim (confirmed by direct comparison), with SetCollapsed() producing an observable visual toggle (TextBox vs TextBlock visibility swap), not a no-op property. RichTextBoxExtensions.GetPlainText() correctly serializes BlockUIContainer-hosted EmbeddedCodeRegionBlocks via ToFencedText() and leaves plain paragraphs unaffected. EmbeddedCodeRegionTests.cs exercises the real production classes (verified via csproj linked-file diff, not a reimplementation) end-to-end and concretely covers both normative contract examples plus a no-regression case. No language-picker UI was added \u2014 Language is purely a settable string property, consistent with scope. Ran the targeted filter myself: 45/45 passed, exactly matching the claim. Ran the full suite twice: one run showed 4 failures matching the claimed count/names exactly (7744/7748); a second run showed one apparent flake in the same pre-existing DPI/pixel-rendering tests, confirming no new regression. One minor, non-blocking nit: EmbeddedCodeRegionBlock.Language (string) hides FrameworkElement.Language (CultureInfo) producing CS0108, which should ideally use \u0027new\u0027 or a renamed property, but it causes no functional defect and is unrelated to task-owned scope.",
